# 5. FEATURE PRESERVATION — THE CRITICAL REDESIGN SAFEGUARD

A redesign must not silently reduce product functionality.

Before substantially changing a module, page, navigation structure, or workflow, identify its meaningful existing capabilities.

Maintain a practical feature-parity record:

| Existing capability | Current behavior | New interface location | Preservation status |
|---|---|---|---|
| Existing feature | Verified behavior | New location or interaction | Preserved / Improved / Blocked / Unverified |

A capability may be:

- Preserved in its existing location.
- Moved to a more understandable location.
- Consolidated with another genuinely equivalent capability.
- Improved without changing its underlying business behavior.
- Deferred because of a verified technical dependency.

Moving or consolidating a feature must not silently remove its functionality, permissions, important data, or established workflow.

Do not mistake a visually simplified page for a successfully simplified product.

Do not hide advanced functionality in a way that makes it difficult to discover.

Do not delete an apparently unused feature without verifying its actual dependencies and usage implications.

Any proposed removal of an important capability requires explicit user approval.

**Feature parity is a required acceptance criterion for every substantial redesign.**

