# 18. ACCEPTANCE GATES

A substantial redesign must pass the relevant gates before being described as complete.

### GATE 1 — FUNCTIONAL PARITY
Important previous capabilities remain available and usable, or explicitly approved changes have been made.

### GATE 2 — JOURNEY INTEGRITY
The affected user journey works from beginning to end.

### GATE 3 — NAVIGATION INTEGRITY
Relevant routes, refresh, direct access, and browser navigation behave correctly.

### GATE 4 — STATE CONTINUITY
Important valid user state is preserved appropriately without exposing sensitive or stale information.

### GATE 5 — VISUAL CONSISTENCY
The implementation follows the shared design system and does not introduce arbitrary visual inconsistencies.

### GATE 6 — RESPONSIVENESS
Affected screens remain usable across relevant device sizes.

### GATE 7 — ACCESSIBILITY
Relevant accessibility issues are addressed and remaining limitations are disclosed.

### GATE 8 — TECHNICAL VERIFICATION
Required available checks are executed and their actual results are recorded.

### GATE 9 — REGRESSION SAFETY
No unresolved critical regression introduced by the change remains undisclosed.

A gate that cannot be verified must be marked unverified, not silently treated as passed.

